content:
CryLog output is not observable from outside the running editor: the log handle stays open
and unflushed, and editor.log's mtime goes stale. The bridge only captures what is logged
during the single command it is executing, so per-frame logging is invisible.

Solution used throughout this project - write diagnostics to a plain file with C stdio,
opening and closing each time:

    FILE* f = nullptr;
    if (fopen_s(&f, "<engine source checkout>", "a") == 0 && f != nullptr)
    {
        fprintf(f, "%s\n", buffer);
        fclose(f);
    }

That file is then staged into the container and read normally. See NpcTrace() in Npc.cpp.

For high-frequency data (every frame), buffer in memory and write once at the end instead
- otherwise the logging becomes the performance problem you are trying to measure. See
CPlayerComponent::FlushWalkTrace.

Npc.cpp: GetOrCreateComponent<CAdvancedAnimationComponent> flagged NoSave; SetMeshType(EMeshType::Render) (no extra
collider -- physics dump still one capsule part); SetCharacterFile(sdk_player.cdf); SetMannequinAnimationDatabaseFile;
SetControllerDefinitionFile; SetDefaultScopeContextName("MainCharacter"); SetDefaultFragmentName("Idle");
SetAnimationDrivenMotion(TRUE) -- CORRECTED 2026-09-16: false was wrong. On the skeleton the flag means "extract root
motion" (SkeletonPose_Process.cpp:100 zeroes the root joint; SkeletonAnim_Locator.cpp:95 computes the locator delta);
the default component never applies that delta to the entity, so navigation + physics still move the NPC. With false
the root keeps the clip's travel: the body walks ahead and snaps back every loop (user-visible "teleports back";
logged animation root speed 0.00; with true 2.00). LoadFromDisk; GetFragmentId("Idle"/"Walk");
ResetCharacter. Before re-physicalizing on game start: ResetCharacter() then controller Physicalize() (ThirdPersonShooter
order). Each update: Walk if measured controller ground speed > 0.3 m/s else Idle, QueueFragmentWithId only on change.
The component itself sets TravelSpeed/TurnAngle/TravelAngle from pe_status_dynamics every frame
(AdvancedAnimationComponent.cpp ~112-137): measured walk blend-space MoveSpeed == ground speed (1.31/1.31, 2.20/2.20).
Patrol unchanged with the body: ~2.15 m/s level/uphill/downhill, 0% flying, 0 path failures.

CPointLightComponent::m_radius is protected (10 m, Schematyc 'Radius'): inside a component of your own it cannot be set, and a 10 m light with no shadows would light the street through the walls.
Mirror what the component does: SRenderLight light; light.SetPosition(ZERO); light.m_Flags = DLF_DEFERRED_LIGHT | DLF_POINT; light.SetLightColor(ColorF(1,0.72,0.45) * k); light.SetSpecularMult(0.15);
light.m_fHDRDynamic = 0; light.SetRadius(radius, bulb); entity->UpdateLightClipBounds(light); slot = entity->LoadLight(-1, &light); free with FreeSlot(slot) before reloading.
The falloff beyond the bulb is (bulb/d)^2 and inside it is flat, so brightness has to scale with the room's AREA: k = 0.13 x radius^2 looked right (cottage 3.9 m: 2.0, tavern 5.5 m: 3.9);
ash_lamp_scale (a CVar on the lamp component) finds the number fast in game. 14 burnt the frame black. A day level (22 %) keeps the room readable through windows; 'all lamps out' must include it.

Music (ash_music_*.ogg, 44.1 kHz stereo vorbis ~150 kbps): menu 116 s, day 114 s, night 124 s, danger 120 s, end 114 s; integrated -11.2, -11.4, -15.5, -13.9, -10.9 LUFS; true peak -0.4 to -1.5 dBFS, no clipped sample;
loop seam 0.01x to 1.6x a typical step (continuous), but the day/end/menu loops START 3 to 5.5 dB louder than they end (a level change across the seam, within normal swell). Voices (vox_*.ogg, Piper, 22 kHz): -16.8 to
-18.5 LUFS, voiced share 0.69-0.78, f0 119-125 Hz for Father Edmund. A FIRST READING WAS WRONG: it took the `0.33f` in Soundscape.cpp's layer table for a gain and concluded the music sits ~10 dB under the dialogue; it is the layer's EASING SPEED ('levels per second'), RETRACTED. What the code and the audio library say: the music option defaults to 0.6 (ash_music, a linear parameter), the music triggers are -5/-6 dB, the voice triggers -3 dB with the master volume, and the music layers are eased down by `duck` (Soundscape.cpp:346): x0.6 in phase 3 = Dialogue, x0.5 in phase 4 = Paused. Rough estimate at defaults (summing dB, not a measurement): day music in play -11.4 -6 -4.4 = about -22 LUFS, under a conversation another -4.4 = about -26 LUFS, against speech -18 -3 = about -21 LUFS: speech about 5 LU above the music while someone talks, about equal when nobody does. MEASURED 2026-10-07 at the real output (meter_probe.py on the Release build, audio_peakmeter.ps1; silence solo 0 reads 0.0000 before and after = the control): day music alone mean 0.197 / peak 0.256; one speech line (Father Edmund, ash_vox_edmund_2, mid-line) alone mean 0.237 / peak 0.621; both together mean 0.397 / peak 0.717 (close to the sum of the parts). So the voice already sits ~1.6 dB above the music on the mean and ~7.7 dB on the peaks, unducked; with the dialogue duck (x0.6 = -4.4 dB on the music) about 6 dB on the mean: the estimate above (speech about 5 LU over the music while someone talks) holds. One line, one voice, a peak meter (not loudness): not a mix study. Files measured with audio_report.py, validated on synthetic signals (a -20 dBFS 1 kHz sine reads -20.0 dBFS, -23.0 dBFS RMS, -23.0 LUFS).
